# Washes with pigment pooled at the edges.
#
# The wash is a small median followed by a wide surface blur held to a *tight*
# threshold. That pairing is the whole thing, and the threshold is the part
# that matters: it is the tolerance within which pixels are allowed to average,
# so a small one flattens the inside of a leaf while refusing to average across
# its edge. Open it up and the radius stops being reach and starts being smear
# — the picture turns to mush and no amount of colour afterwards rescues it.
#
# The second layer is the pooling: edges multiplied back at low strength,
# standing in for the dark rim that forms where a wet edge dries. It has to be
# sparse. A dense edge map blurred and multiplied is not pooling, it is dirt.

param flow = 0.09
param edge = 0.35
param bleed = 0.3

filter median radius={min*0.0045}
filter surface-blur radius={min*0.0295} threshold={flow}
adjust vibrance vibrance=0.35
adjust levels out-black=0.04 out-white=0.99 gamma=1.04

place
adjust black-and-white
filter find-edges
adjust levels black=0.04 white=0.46
filter gaussian-blur radius={min*0.0015+bleed}
set name="Pooling" blend="Multiply" opacity={edge}
layer flatten
